{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially during rising regimes. Apply an upward level shift (≈ +1 to +3 EUR) as baseline, larger when trend is up.",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: after a downside inflection in history, the model projects sideways-to-down while the market rebounds strongly. Add a positive trend-following correction when the last 5–10 days show stabilization after a dip.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors grow with horizon; days ~10–25 are most underpredicted during rallies. Use a ramping correction that increases with lead time (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Missed rally amplitude: during sustained upswings, the model caps prices around ~69–71 while outcomes reach ~72–73. Introduce a volatility/convexity uplift when recent realized volatility is elevated and momentum is positive.",
    "Late-horizon overshoot risk after sharp peaks: when the model finally turns bullish late in the window, it can overshoot if the market mean-reverts (seen in the case where realized drops while forecast stays high). Add a mean-reversion dampener beyond ~day 20 when the forecast implies a continued steep rise but recent spot has started to roll over."
  ]
}
